	abstract = {Source code clone detection is a critical research area in software engineering, playing a vital role in code quality management and software maintenance.Over the years, this field has attracted increasing attention from both academia and industry. Existing approaches for clone detection primarily fall into two categories: graph-based methods and text-based methods. However, graph-based techniques often struggle to detect Type III clones due to their reliance on structural similarity, while text-based methods exhibit limitations in identifying Type IV clones owing to their inability to capture deep semantic relationships. To bridge this gap, in this paper we propose Source Code clOne deTection based on semanTic mining from graph and text data (SCOTT), which detects all types of code clone patterns in an end-to-end manner. We conduct extensive experiments on the benchmark datasets of Big-CloneBench, POJ-104 and CF-500, and results show that SCOTT can averagely achieve 7.8% higher Hits@k and 5.6% higher MRR@k compared with the state-of-the-art methods. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights are reserved, including those for text and data mining, AI training, and similar technologies.},
