	abstract = {Context: Unit testing plays a vital role in the software development process. However, manually creating unit test cases is tedious and error-prone, making automated generation a persistent research focus. Various approaches have been proposed, but each has its own strengths and limitations. Objectives: This paper systematically reviews recent advances in automated unit test case generation over the past five years. It aims to statistically analyze the research trends of various methodologies, summarize the enhancements and technical specifics of mainstream techniques, with particular emphasis on search-based and deep learning-based approaches, and identify key challenges from multiple perspectives, ultimately proposing promising directions for future research. Methods: We collected 171 relevant papers published in the last five years, conducted a quantitative analysis, and selected 67 high-quality studies for detailed assessment. Results: We found that, over the past five years, search-based methods have received the most research attention, accounting for approximately 54% of the analyzed studies. Since 2023, approaches based on large language models (LLMs) have emerged and quickly gained popularity, representing about 24% of the studies. Some approaches that combine search-based methods with LLMs have achieved better results, showing a growing trend toward combining multiple techniques. Conclusion: Although automated unit test case generation has made substantial advances, challenges remain in test quality, execution efficiency, and real-world applicability. Future research could continue exploring innovations in generation methods and performance optimization, for example, by leveraging the semantic understanding capabilities of LLMs to mitigate the limitations of search-based, symbolic execution, and similar methods, or by combining multiple methods to fully exploit the advantages of each approach. In addition, evaluating test generation methods ac