	affiliations = {University of Detroit Mercy, Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering and Computer Science, Detroit, 48221, MI, United States},
	abstract = {This article presents a rigorous empirical study on prompt recovery, the task of inferring the original natural language instruction from a given source code snippet. We evaluate four state-of-the-art Large Language Models (LLMs): OpenAI's GPT-4o-mini, Google's Gemini-2.5-Flash, Meta's llama3.2:3B, and DeepSeek's DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B models on their ability to recover prompts from Python code. The analysis also investigates the impact of code degradation (via variable renaming and comment removal), which serves as a proxy for poorly documented legacy systems. Additionally a preliminary study of generative sampling temperature effects was conducted using OpenAI's GPT model. We evaluate performance using a comprehensive suite of metrics, including BLEU, ROUGE, BERTScore, and Levenshtein Distance. Our findings reveal that while code cleaning degrades lexical similarity scores (e.g., average BLEU scores for OpenAI are 20% lower), it consistently maintains semantic similarity (e.g., average BERT-F1 remains above 0.90). This suggests that in the absence of lexical cues, models are forced to rely on deeper algorithmic reasoning and structural understanding of source code. Comparative analysis shows cloud-hosted models exhibit superior semantic fidelity, while DeepSeek-R1 demonstrates competitive character-level accuracy. These results provide critical insights into the code comprehension capabilities of modern LLMs and have significant implications for their application in reverse engineering, legacy system documentation, and the development of more robust evaluation benchmarks for code intelligence.  © 2013 IEEE.},
