	abstract = {Within the rapid change of software engineering field, a key challenge is the translation of the high -level models to executable codes. The present study introduces "Bridging Models and Code,"which is a new visual tool that has been designed for automatically converting the class domain models to structured and executable codes in Java, C#, and Python programming languages. Utilizing the principles of MDD (i.e., model-driven development), the tool can take structural as well as behavioral information the from class models then it creates well-organized code skeletons, including class definitions, methods, relationships, and variables. Those skeletons provide the developers with some strong starting point for the further customization in order to meet some particular needs of the project. Through the automation of this code generation part, the tool is helpful in speeding up the early stages of development, promoting consistency across a variety of the programming languages, and reducing the manual work that is involved usually in the turning of the models to software architecture. The present study provides an evaluation of the performance of the tool through the comparison of the outputs from a variety of the domain models in the 3 languages, focusing upon how well it can maintain the structure as well as the adaptability. Results have shown that the tool can be effective in the addressing of the challenges in the generation of the multi-language codes, preserving the accuracy of the design, and reducing the likelihood of the errors that can often happen with the manual coding. © 2026 Author(s).},
