	affiliations = {Department of Computer Science, National Taichung University of Education, Taichung, Taiwan},
	abstract = {In recent years, Large Language Models (LLMs) have gained attention and been widely applied, but research on their use in evaluating software engineering assignments remains limited. Therefore, we propose using LLMs to automate software engineering assignment evaluation, reducing teachers' workload and avoiding subjectively assessing students' assignments. On the other hand, when applying LLMs, it is crucial to have good prompts to guide the model's interpretation and analysis of natural language input. Thus, in this research, we design a series of experiments to evaluate the students' assignments, in which essential parameters are considered, including the design of prompts during the assessment process for better responses, the preprocess of input images to filter out unqualified assignments for better performance, the conversion of assignments into text formats to improve the understanding of the content, the comparison of the results from various threads of assessments for finding out discrepancies, and the analysis of those discrepancies for further refining the prompts. We conducted the experiments using assignments from an advanced software engineering course in graduate school and OpenAI's GPT-4o mini model. The results show that the proposed method reduces subjectivity in scoring. © The Institution of Engineering & Technology 2025.},
