	affiliations = {Infocomm Technology Cluster, Singapore Institute of Technology, Singapore; School of Computing Science, University of Glasgow, United Kingdom},
	abstract = {This innovative practice full paper explores how just-in-time teaching can improve students' practical skills in Git and project management tools within undergraduate software engineering courses. Many students struggle with applying GitHub workflows confidently, often resorting to self-learning via inconsistent or unreliable sources, including large language models. To address this, we designed and evaluated an instructional model that combines short, instructor-curated video tutorials, guided hands-on exercises, and immediate formative quizzes. These modules cover essential Git commands (git init, branching, merges), collaborative workflows (pull requests, project boards), and best practices. Our findings suggest that this just-in-time approach supports strong short-term knowledge acquisition, with students performing well in topic-aligned quizzes. However, some confidence gaps remain in complex topics, such as GitHub Projects and advanced. gitignore usage. These results highlight the potential of just-in-time, quiz-embedded instruction while suggesting the need for extended team-based practice to support deeper mastery. © 2025 IEEE.},
