	abstract = {The advent of artificial intelligence has introduced significant possibilities in the field of software testing automation, offering efficiency gains and enhanced accuracy through innovative methodologies. In this paper, we present a novel automated system designed to extract object locators from Delphi-based applications, utilizing a robust combination of generative AI techniques and advanced prompt engineering strategies. Our approach centers around the fine-tuning of a large language model, Mistral-7B, which, when paired with strategic prompt engineering, demonstrates unprecedented accuracy in identifying and mapping user interface elements within Delphi codebases.As software development cycles continue to accelerate, the imperative to reduce time-intensive manual testing processes has never been more crucial. The system significantly mitigates the labor-intensive nature of manual locator extraction, proposing a scalable solution capable of adapting to varying code structures. Experimentation reveals that our model not only enhances the automation of UI element mapping but also provides insights into improving the adaptability and rigor of software testing processes. Through this work, we highlight the potential for AI-driven automation to revolutionize testing workflows, promoting efficiency and reducing human error.Furthermore, we explore the feasibility of extending our automated system to incorporate web-based applications, enabling the automatic extraction of HTML elements and object locators. This expansion underscores our broader vision of developing a unified test automation framework applicable across multiple platforms, ultimately fostering innovation and optimization in software testing paradigms. Our findings contribute a substantial forward leap in the automation of test processes, presenting a transformative tool for practitioners and researchers alike.  © 2025 IEEE.},
