	affiliations = {SAP Security Research, Karlsruhe, Germany; Technische Universität Berlin, Berlin, Germany; Max Planck Institute, Bochum, Germany},
	abstract = {Several learning-based vulnerability detection methods have been proposed to assist developers during the secure software development life-cycle. In particular, recent learning-based large transformer networks have shown remarkably high performance in various vulnerability detection and localization benchmarks. However, these models have also been shown to have difficulties accurately locating the root cause of flaws and generalizing to out-of-distribution samples. In this work, we investigate this problem and identify spurious correlations as the main obstacle to transferability and generalization, resulting in performance losses of up to 30% for current models. We propose a method to measure the impact of these spurious correlations on learning models and estimate their true, unbiased performance. We present several strategies to counteract the underlying confounding bias, but ultimately our work highlights the limitations of evaluations in the laboratory for complex learning tasks such as vulnerability discovery.  © 2023 Owner/Author.},
